\title{sphereg2: Kernel Ridge Regression for Spherical and Elastic Shape Responses}
\description{
Nonparametric regression for responses taking values on the unit sphere of a real separable Hilbert space, with covariates in an arbitrary Polish space, and its extension to planar shapes compared elastically: the elastic kernel ridge regression of Matteo, Stöcker, Pigoli and Tavakoli (2026) \doi{10.48550/arXiv.2610.08386}. The package builds on 'sphereg', the implementation of the spherical kernel ridge regression of Matteo, Stöcker and Tavakoli (2026) \doi{10.48550/arXiv.2606.00181}: the conditional Fréchet mean is modelled intrinsically as the exponential map of an element of a vector-valued reproducing kernel Hilbert space, and estimated by minimising a penalised empirical risk built from squared geodesic distances. The geometry is supplied by the user as an inner product, so the same code handles directional and compositional data in finite dimensions as well as square-root densities and other functional responses in infinite dimensions; Karhunen-Loève coordinates and low-rank kernel approximations keep the high-dimensional case tractable. Building on the square-root velocity function (SRVF) framework of Srivastava and Klassen (2016) \doi{10.1007/978-1-4939-4020-2}, responses may also be planar shapes analysed elastically, in which case the fit alternates with an alignment of the responses over rotations and reparametrisations, so that the estimated mean is a conditional mean with respect to the elastic shape distance. Tangent-space and unconstrained vector-valued kernel ridge regression are included for comparison, together with cross-validation routines.
}
